1
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:49,661
<v ADAM> OK Rosie, shall we try to get some ideas together for our presentation on diet and obesity?

2
00:00:49,671 --> 00:00:50,826
<v ROSIE> Sure.

3
00:00:50,836 --> 00:00:58,081
<v ADAM> I can talk about the experiment I did to see if people can tell the difference between real sugar and artificial sweeteners.

4
00:00:58,091 --> 00:01:04,728
<v ROSIE> Where you gave people drinks with either sugar or artificial sweeteners and they had to say which they thought it was?

5
00:01:04,738 --> 00:01:14,190
<v ADAM> Yeah. It took me ages to decide exactly how I'd organise it, especially how I could make sure that people didn't know which drink I was giving them.

6
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:24,551
<v ADAM> It was hard to keep track of it all, especially as I had so many people doing it - I had to make sure I kept a proper record of what each person had had.

7
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:27,361
<v ROSIE> So could most people tell the difference?

8
00:01:27,371 --> 00:01:32,063
<v ADAM> Yeah - I hadn't thought they would be able to, but most people could.

9
00:01:32,073 --> 00:01:41,939
<v ROSIE> Then there's that experiment I did measuring the fat content of nuts, to see if the nutritional information given on the packet was accurate.

10
00:01:41,949 --> 00:01:47,077
<v ADAM> The one where you ground up the nuts and mixed them with a chemical to absorb the fat?

11
00:01:47,087 --> 00:01:56,000
<v ROSIE> Yes. My results were a bit problematic - the fat content for that type of nut seemed much lower than it said on the package.

12
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,990
<v ROSIE> But I reckon the package information was right.

13
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:03,230
<v ROSIE> I think I should probably have ground up the nuts more than I did.

14
00:02:03,230 --> 00:02:08,039
<v ROSIE> It's possible that the scales for weighing the fat weren't accurate enough, too.

15
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:11,700
<v ROSIE> I'd really like to try the experiment again some time.

16
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:17,330
<v ADAM> So what can we say about helping people to lose weight?

17
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:21,910
<v ADAM> There's a lot we could say about what restaurants could do to reduce obesity.

18
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:30,820
<v ADAM> I read that the items at the start of a menu and the items at the end of a menu are much more likely to be chosen than the items in the middle.

19
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:42,424
<v ADAM> So, if you put the low-calorie items at the beginning and end of the menu, people will probably go for the food with fewer calories, without even realising what they're doing. 

20
00:03:42,434 --> 00:03:47,408
<v ROSIE> I think food manufacturers could do more to encourage healthy eating.

21
00:03:47,418 --> 00:03:48,224
<v ADAM> How?

22
00:03:48,234 --> 00:03:57,350
<v ROSIE> Well, when manufacturers put calorie counts of a food on the label, they're sometimes really confusing and I suspect they do it on purpose.

23
00:03:57,350 --> 00:04:02,852
<v ROSIE> Because food that's high in calories tastes better, and so they'll sell more.

24
00:04:02,862 --> 00:04:12,260
<v ADAM> Yeah, so if you look at the amount of calories in a pizza, they'll give you the calories per quarter pizza and you think, oh that's not too bad.

25
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:14,943
<v ADAM> But who's going to eat a quarter pizza?

26
00:04:14,953 --> 00:04:16,110
<v ROSIE> Exactly

27
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:21,392
<v ADAM> I suppose another approach to this problem is to get people to exercise more.

28
00:04:21,402 --> 00:04:29,120
<v ROSIE> Right. In England, the current guidelines are for at least 30 minutes of brisk walking, five days a week.

29
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:48,908
<v ROSIE> Now when you ask them, about 40% of men and 30% of women say they do this, but when you objectively measure the amount of walking they do with motion sensors, you find that only 6% of men and 4% of women do the recommended amount of exercise.

30
00:04:48,918 --> 00:04:52,230
<v ADAM> Mm, so you can see why obesity is growing.

31
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:56,870
<v ROSIE> So how can people be encouraged to take more exercise?

32
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:02,430
<v ADAM> Well, for example, think of the location of stairs in a train station.

33
00:05:02,430 --> 00:05:10,770
<v ADAM> If people reach the stairs before they reach the escalator when they're leaving the station, they're more likely to take the stairs.

34
00:05:10,770 --> 00:05:17,170
<v ADAM> And if you increase the width of the stairs, you'll get more people using them at the same time.

35
00:05:17,170 --> 00:05:23,124
<v ADAM> It's an unconscious process and influenced by minor modifications in their environment.

36
00:05:23,134 --> 00:05:29,730
<v ROSIE> Right. And it might not be a big change, but if it happens every day, it all adds up.

37
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:35,312
<v ADAM> Yes. But actually, I'm not sure if we should be talking about exercise in our presentation.

38
00:05:35,322 --> 00:05:38,556
<v ROSIE> Well, we've done quite a bit of reading about it.

39
00:05:38,566 --> 00:05:49,003
<v ADAM> I know, but it's going to mean we have a very wide focus, and our tutor did say that we need to focus on causes and solutions in terms of nutrition.

40
00:05:49,013 --> 00:05:50,350
<v ROSIE> I suppose so.

41
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:53,690
<v ROSIE> And we've got plenty of information about that.

42
00:05:53,690 --> 00:05:56,975
<v ROSIE> OK, well that will be simpler.

43
00:05:56,985 --> 00:05:59,440
<v ADAM> So what shall we do now?

44
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:02,454
<v ADAM> We've still got half an hour before our next lecture.

45
00:06:02,464 --> 00:06:06,750
<v ROSIE> Let's think about what we're going to include and what will go where.

46
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:09,502
<v ROSIE> Then we can decide what slides we need. 

47
00:06:09,512 --> 00:06:11,214
<v ADAM> OK, fine.